    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/paolo-lucio-anafesto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paolo-lucio-anafesto.jpg" alt="Paolo Lucio Anafesto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/paolo-lucio-anafesto">Paolo Lucio Anafesto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_vita_ducum_Venetorum_1574.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jost Amman, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> grew out of the settlements that mainland Venetians founded on the islands of their lagoon from the fifth and sixth centuries, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, who invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 568, made the old Roman cities of the region unsafe. The lagoon communities formed part of the Byzantine province centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Ravenna</a>, governed by tribunes and later by a <em>dux</em>, or doge. Venetian tradition places the election of the first doge, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-lucio-anafesto">Paolo Lucio Anafesto</a>, in 697, though his existence is doubtful and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orso-ipato">Orso Ipato</a>, elected in 726, is the first doge securely attested. When the Lombard kingdom fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, Venice lay on the frontier between the Carolingian and Byzantine worlds. In 810 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-italy">Pepin of Italy</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, attacked the lagoon and overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chioggia">Chioggia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pellestrina">Pellestrina</a> but failed to subdue the central islands, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-aachen-812">Peace of Aachen</a> of 812 left Venice under nominal Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. The seat of government then moved to the more defensible <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rialto">Rialto</a> islands under Doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnello-participazio">Agnello Participazio</a>. In 828 two Venetian merchants smuggled the relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist">Saint Mark the Evangelist</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, and the saint became the symbol of a city that increasingly acted as an independent state, recognised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pactum-lotharii">Pactum Lotharii</a> of 840.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/venice"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venice.jpg" alt="Venice" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venezia_aerial_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kallerna, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> lived by trade. Its merchants carried grain, salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and slaves in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and later became the principal intermediaries between western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, importing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and alum from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and selling them on through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fondaco-dei-tedeschi">Fondaco dei Tedeschi</a> to merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. A chrysobull of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> in 1082 granted Venetians tax-free trade throughout the empire. From the fourteenth century the state auctioned the use of its own merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>, which sailed in regulated convoys called <em>mude</em> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, Alexandria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and elsewhere. The gold ducat, first minted in 1284, became one of the standard coins of the Mediterranean. Industry flourished: shipbuilding at the Arsenal, glass-making, concentrated on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murano">Murano</a> from 1291, silk weaving, soap, and from 1469 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius">Aldus Manutius</a> making Venice the leading centre of the European book trade around 1500. The city probably held more than 100,000 people before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1348 and again in the sixteenth century. Society was divided into the hereditary nobility, the privileged <em>cittadini</em> who staffed the chancery, and the popolani, organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> and confraternities called <em>scuole</em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agnadello"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-agnadello.jpg" alt="Battle of Agnadello" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agnadello">Battle of Agnadello</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_d%27Agnadel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pierre-Jules Jollivet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Venetian power rested on the fleet. Around 1000 Doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-ii-orseolo">Pietro II Orseolo</a> led an expedition that brought the Dalmatian coast under Venetian protection, an event commemorated in the annual ceremony of the marriage of the sea. The Arsenal, traditionally founded in 1104 and greatly enlarged in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries, became one of the largest industrial complexes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, able to build and fit out war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> in a standardised sequence. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venetians</a> transported the crusading army, diverted it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zara</a> in 1202 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, which fell in 1204; Doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enrico-dandolo">Enrico Dandolo</a> secured Venice a claim to three-eighths of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Long rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> culminated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-chioggia">War of Chioggia</a> of 1378–1381, when a Genoese fleet occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chioggia">Chioggia</a> inside the lagoon before being blockaded and forced to surrender. On land the republic relied on hired condottieri such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-bussone-da-carmagnola">Francesco Bussone</a>, called Carmagnola, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeo-colleoni">Bartolomeo Colleoni</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-cambrai">League of Cambrai</a> crushed its army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agnadello">Battle of Agnadello</a> in 1509, though Venice recovered most of its mainland. Against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> it fought a series of wars from 1463, contributed about half the galleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571">Holy League</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> in 1571, defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Candia</a> for over twenty years until 1669, and conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-morosini">Francesco Morosini</a> in 1684–1687.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marino-faliero"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marino-faliero.jpg" alt="Marino Faliero" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marino-faliero">Marino Faliero</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bolla_Marino_Falier_-_Le_monete_di_Venezia_(page_205_crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Niccolò Papadopoli, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> was an aristocratic republic. The doge, elected for life, was surrounded from the twelfth century by councils that steadily reduced him to a dignified chief magistrate bound by an oath of office, the <em>promissione</em>; Doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marino-faliero">Marino Faliero</a> was beheaded in 1355 for plotting to seize power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> lay with the Great Council, which the reform known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serrata-del-maggior-consiglio">Serrata</a> of 1297 restricted to families already represented in it, creating a closed hereditary patriciate whose members were later registered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libro-doro">Golden Book</a>. The Senate directed foreign policy, trade and war; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/collegio-venice">Collegio</a> prepared business; and the Council of Ten, set up after the conspiracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bajamonte-tiepolo">Bajamonte Tiepolo</a> in 1310, guarded state security with secret procedures. Doges were chosen by an elaborate sequence of lot and election designed to prevent faction. Offices were short and rotated among nobles, and an extensive chancery staffed by <em>cittadini</em> kept the records, while Venetian ambassadors’ reports, the <em>relazioni</em>, became famous for their detail. Overseas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stato-da-mar">Stato da Mar</a> was ruled by appointed governors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, Corfu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and, from 1489, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. The mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domini-di-terraferma">Terraferma</a>, conquered between 1339 and 1428, including Treviso, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>, Verona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> and Bergamo, kept its own statutes and local councils under Venetian rectors. The stability of this system impressed political writers across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/florence"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florence.jpg" alt="Florence" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firenze_-_Piazzale_Michelangelo,_Firenze,_Italy_-_April_6,_2015_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Giorgio Galeotti, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For several centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> was the main commercial link between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Europe</a> and the Byzantine and Islamic East, and its merchants shaped the trade of the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Venetian commercial practice, including partnership contracts, state-regulated shipping, double-entry bookkeeping and the public debt, influenced business across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the city produced travellers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>, whose account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> was widely read. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, steered in part by Venetian interests, gravely weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and spread Venetian colonies and Italian culture across the Greek world, where Venetian fortresses and architecture still stand from Kotor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. Venice was also one of the great artistic centres of Europe: its school of painting included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-bellini">Giovanni Bellini</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgione">Giorgione</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tintoretto">Tintoretto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-veronese">Paolo Veronese</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-palladio">Andrea Palladio</a> built churches and villas that were copied for centuries, and composers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudio-monteverdi">Claudio Monteverdi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-vivaldi">Antonio Vivaldi</a> worked in the city, which opened the first public opera house in 1637. Venetian printers spread Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> classics in affordable editions. The republic’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixed-constitution">mixed constitution</a> was admired by political thinkers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> as a model of stable, law-bound government, the ‘myth of Venice’. In its long decline it became a centre of tourism and pleasure, and the city remains one of the most visited places in the world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz.jpg" alt="Treaty of Passarowitz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tamis_banat1718_1739.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WikiEditor2004, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> was gradual. The advance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> cost it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a> in 1470, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a> in 1500, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in 1571 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> in 1669, while the opening of the Portuguese sea route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> after 1498 and the later rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English shipping eroded its position in the spice trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Peace of Passarowitz</a> of 1718 confirmed the loss of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> and left the republic with only the Ionian Islands and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and Albania overseas. In the eighteenth century Venice pursued a policy of strict neutrality, maintained a reduced fleet and remained wealthy, but it no longer counted as a major power. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>’s army invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1796, the republic tried to stay neutral, but after anti-French risings on the mainland and the firing on a French ship at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lido-di-venezia">Lido</a>, Napoleon declared war. On 12 May 1797 the Great Council voted to dissolve the republic, and the last doge, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-manin">Ludovico Manin</a>, abdicated. A short-lived provisional municipality followed, but by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> in October 1797 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> handed Venice, Istria and Dalmatia to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg Monarchy</a>. After further changes of ruler under Napoleon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, Venice joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1866.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> built a thousand-year state on trade and sea power, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Europe</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the Islamic East, ruling a chain of colonies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and offering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> both a celebrated model of stable aristocratic government and one of its richest artistic cultures.</em></p>

